---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout title="2.2 Organizational Structure">
  <div class="breadcrumb">
    <a href="/ibbm/">Home</a><span class="sep">›</span>
    <a href="/ibbm/unit2/">Unit 2</a><span class="sep">›</span>
    <span>2.2 Organizational Structure</span>
  </div>
  <div class="topic-hero">
    <span class="topic-number">2.2</span>
    <h1>Organizational Structure</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">How businesses organize people, who makes the decisions, and what can go wrong.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="tabs">
    <button class="tab-btn active" onclick="switchTab(this,'notes')">Notes</button>
    <button class="tab-btn" onclick="switchTab(this,'worked')">Worked Examples</button>
    <button class="tab-btn" onclick="switchTab(this,'practice')">Practice</button>
  </div>

  <!-- NOTES -->
  <div id="notes" class="tab-content active">

    <div class="learning-goals">
      <div class="learning-goals-title">Learning Goals</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Define and explain delegation, span of control, levels of hierarchy, chain of command, bureaucracy, centralization, decentralization, delayering and matrix structure</li>
        <li>Read and draw flat, tall, and product, function or region charts</li>
        <li>Judge which structure suits a change in external factors</li>
        <li>Explain project-based organization and Handy's Shamrock <span class="hl-badge">HL</span></li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Key terms</h2>
      <div class="key-terms">
        <div class="key-term-card"><div class="term">Delegation</div><div class="definition">Passing authority to make a decision down to a subordinate, who stays answerable for the result.</div></div>
        <div class="key-term-card"><div class="term">Span of control</div><div class="definition">The number of people who report directly to one manager.</div></div>
        <div class="key-term-card"><div class="term">Levels of hierarchy</div><div class="definition">The number of ranks of authority from top to bottom.</div></div>
        <div class="key-term-card"><div class="term">Chain of command</div><div class="definition">The line of authority that instructions pass down, and reports pass up.</div></div>
        <div class="key-term-card"><div class="term">Bureaucracy</div><div class="definition">Heavy rules, procedures and paperwork, so decisions take many approvals.</div></div>
        <div class="key-term-card"><div class="term">Centralization</div><div class="definition">Decisions kept at the top.</div></div>
        <div class="key-term-card"><div class="term">Decentralization</div><div class="definition">Decision-making authority spread down and out to lower levels or branches.</div></div>
        <div class="key-term-card"><div class="term">Delayering</div><div class="definition">Removing a level of the hierarchy, which widens spans of control.</div></div>
        <div class="key-term-card"><div class="term">Matrix structure</div><div class="definition">Staff report to their department manager and to a project manager at the same time.</div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="callout exam-tip">
        <div class="callout-title">Tall vs flat</div>
        <p><strong>Tall:</strong> many levels, narrow spans, long chain of command. Tight control, but slow decisions. <strong>Flat:</strong> few levels, wide spans, short chain. Fast and cheaper, but managers can be stretched.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Ways to organize a chart</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>By</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Function</strong></td><td>Marketing, finance, IT and operations departments</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Product</strong></td><td>Separate divisions for shoes, bags and watches</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Region</strong></td><td>One unit for each country the business sells in</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>When external factors change, structure follows: a recession may push delayering, new markets may need regional units, fast-moving rivals may call for decentralization or project teams, and new technology may remove whole layers of admin.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Advantages and disadvantages</h2>
      <div class="callout remember">
        <div class="callout-title">Pending teacher review</div>
        <p>Only the decentralization row comes from the lesson materials. Try Activity 3 in Practice before reading this.</p>
      </div>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Structure</th><th>Advantages</th><th>Disadvantages</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Decentralization</strong></td><td>Faster decisions by the people closest to customers; staff feel trusted and motivated; frees top managers for strategy; develops managers' skills</td><td>Less consistency between branches; harder to control; mistakes by less experienced decision makers</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Bureaucracy</strong></td><td>Clear rules, consistency and control; fewer costly mistakes</td><td>Slow decisions; frustrating and demotivating; inflexible when things change</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Delayering</strong></td><td>Lower costs; shorter chain, so faster communication; more responsibility lower down</td><td>Wider spans can overload managers; job losses hurt morale; fewer promotion steps</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Matrix</strong></td><td>Brings skills from across departments together; flexible for projects</td><td>Two bosses can give conflicting instructions; unclear who owns what</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Changing structures <span class="hl-badge">HL</span></h2>
      <div class="callout remember">
        <div class="callout-title">Pending teacher review</div>
        <p>This section was written from the syllabus to fill a gap in the lesson materials.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="key-terms">
        <div class="key-term-card"><div class="term">Project-based organization</div><div class="definition">Work organized around projects rather than fixed departments. Teams form for a project and break up when it ends.</div></div>
        <div class="key-term-card"><div class="term">Handy's Shamrock</div><div class="definition">Three "leaves" of workers: a small <strong>core</strong> of key permanent staff, a <strong>contractual fringe</strong> of outside specialists hired for specific jobs, and a <strong>flexible workforce</strong> of part-time and temporary staff brought in as demand changes.</div></div>
      </div>
      <p>Both make a business more flexible and cheaper to run, but job security falls for everyone outside the core.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="recap">
      <div class="recap-title">Recap</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Tall: many levels, narrow spans, slow. Flat: few levels, wide spans, fast</li>
        <li>Delayering removes a level; decentralization moves decisions down</li>
        <li>Matrix gives staff two bosses: flexible, but confusing</li>
        <li>Charts can be by function, product or region; structure should change when external factors do</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div><!-- /notes -->

  <!-- WORKED EXAMPLES -->
  <div id="worked" class="tab-content">
    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Worked Example: Lotus Coffee</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">Lotus Coffee opened its first café in Ho Chi Minh City in 2015. The founder, Vy, made every decision herself: the menu, the suppliers, the staff rosters and even the price of a latte. By 2020 there were 40 cafés and she could no longer keep up.</p>
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">The company now had five ranks of authority: Vy, three directors, eight area managers, 40 café managers and the baristas. Each area manager looked after five café managers, and each café manager supervised about six baristas. When a coffee machine broke, the barista told the café manager, who told the area manager, who told a director, who finally approved the repair. Repairs took nine days. Staff also had to fill in a form for almost everything: a request form to swap a shift, three signatures to buy milk worth more than 500,000 VND, and a written procedure for how to wipe a table.</p>
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">In 2022 Vy changed course. Café managers were given the authority to order milk, hire baristas and repair equipment up to 5 million VND without asking anyone, though they stayed answerable to their bosses for the results. Soon after, the company removed the area manager rank altogether. Café managers now reported straight to the directors, who each looked after about 13 of them. Cafés could also choose their own seasonal drinks instead of waiting for head office to send a menu.</p>
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">In 2024 Lotus built a mobile ordering app. The project team included a barista, a finance officer and a marketing assistant. Each stayed in their usual department and kept their usual manager, but also took daily instructions from Mai, the app project lead, until launch.</p>
        <div class="exercise-q">Find one example from the case for each of the nine terms. (Try it yourself first: cover the table below.)</div>
        <div class="answer-content" style="display:block;">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>In the case</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><strong>Delegation</strong></td><td>Café managers can order milk, hire and repair up to 5 million VND, but stay answerable for results</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Span of control</strong></td><td>Area managers: 5 café managers. Café managers: about 6 baristas. Directors after delayering: about 13</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Levels of hierarchy</strong></td><td>Five ranks: Vy, directors, area managers, café managers, baristas</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Chain of command</strong></td><td>Barista → café manager → area manager → director for a repair, taking nine days</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Bureaucracy</strong></td><td>A form to swap a shift, three signatures for milk, a written procedure for wiping a table</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Centralization</strong></td><td>Vy made every decision herself, even the price of a latte</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Decentralization</strong></td><td>Cafés choose their own seasonal drinks</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Delayering</strong></td><td>The area manager rank was removed</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>Matrix structure</strong></td><td>The app team keep their usual manager but also take instructions from Mai</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div><!-- /worked -->

  <!-- PRACTICE -->
  <div id="practice" class="tab-content">

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 1: Kestrel Foods, reading an org chart</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <a href="/ibbm/img/kestrel-foods-org-chart.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener" title="Open full size"><img src="/ibbm/img/kestrel-foods-org-chart.png" alt="Kestrel Foods organization chart: a CEO; Directors of Operations, Marketing and Finance; Plant, Logistics, Brand, Sales and Accounts Managers; staff below them; and a Project Manager linked by dotted lines to four staff" width="2048" height="1091" style="width:100%; height:auto; margin-bottom:1rem;" /></a>
        <ol style="font-size:0.9rem;">
          <li>How many levels of hierarchy are there? <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Four: CEO, directors, managers, staff</span></button></li>
          <li>Which manager has the widest span of control, and which the narrowest? <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Widest: Plant Manager (5). Narrowest: Director of Finance (1)</span></button></li>
          <li>Trace the chain of command from the CEO to a member of the Plant Manager's team. How many links? <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">CEO → Director of Operations → Plant Manager → staff: 3 links</span></button></li>
          <li>Four people have two managers. Name this structure, and explain one advantage and one disadvantage. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Matrix. Brings skills from operations, marketing, sales and finance together on one project, but two bosses can give conflicting instructions</span></button></li>
          <li>Name one decision the Director of Marketing could delegate to the Brand Manager. Explain one effect on the Brand Manager. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">For example, choosing a packaging design. The Brand Manager feels trusted and builds skills, so is likely to be more motivated</span></button></li>
          <li>If the CEO makes every decision, is Kestrel centralized or decentralized? One effect on the Plant Manager? <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Centralized. The Plant Manager must wait for approval even on factory-floor problems, slowing fixes and reducing motivation</span></button></li>
          <li>Kestrel delayers by removing the manager level. What are the new spans of the directors, and the new chain of command? <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Operations 7, Marketing 7, Finance 2. CEO → director → staff: 2 links</span></button></li>
          <li>Explain two features of the chart that could lead to bureaucracy. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Four levels mean decisions pass through several approvals. Four staff report to two managers, so decisions may need sign-off from both</span></button></li>
        </ol>
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;"><strong>Extension:</strong> evaluate whether delayering is appropriate for Kestrel Foods.</p>
        <div class="callout remember">
          <div class="callout-title">Pending teacher review</div>
          <p>The Kestrel answers are pending teacher review.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 2: Lotus Coffee</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">Read the Lotus Coffee case in the Worked Examples tab. Without looking at the table, write each of the nine terms in your own words and find one example from the case. Then check.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 3: Advantages and disadvantages</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">Without your notes, work out advantages and disadvantages of decentralization, bureaucracy, delayering and a matrix structure. Use Kestrel and Lotus to help. Think about speed of decisions, control, motivation, communication, cost and flexibility. Then check the Notes tab in a different colour and star what you missed. Which surprised you most?</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 4: Uber, a real structure problem</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;"><strong>Uber before the changes.</strong> Uber is a global ride-hailing and delivery company with about 34,000 employees. Some staff sit seven or more reporting layers away from the CEO. Many teams are very small, with only one or two people reporting to a manager. The company runs three separate Delivery Operations groups. Internal staff surveys found that too much work needed cross-team coordination, that decisions were taking too long, and that it was unclear who owned what.</p>
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;"><strong>Part 1: Diagnose</strong></p>
        <ol style="font-size:0.9rem;">
          <li>Seven or more layers: tall or flat? What does it suggest about the chain of command?</li>
          <li>Many managers have one or two direct reports. What does this suggest about span of control, and what might it cost?</li>
          <li>Three separate Delivery Operations groups. How centralized is decision-making at Uber?</li>
          <li>Explain two features of the structure that could make decisions slow.</li>
          <li>How is too much cross-team coordination like the problems of a matrix structure?</li>
          <li>Nobody is sure who owns what. How could delegation be part of the problem?</li>
        </ol>
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;"><strong>Part 2: Anticipate.</strong> Explain two problems a long chain of command could cause for Uber's customers or business, and one problem each for managers and employees. Which is most serious? Justify.</p>
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;"><strong>Part 3: Propose.</strong> Propose two changes to Uber's structure, naming the 2.2 term each uses and the problem it solves. What new problem could each create? Should Uber cut jobs? Justify.</p>
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;"><strong>Part 4: What actually happened.</strong> Read <a href="https://www.fastcompany.com/91601567/the-great-flattening-rolls-on-as-uber-lays-off-middle-managers" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fast Company</a> and <a href="https://www.hcamag.com/au/news/general/uber-slashes-10-of-workforce-to-flatten-management-ranks/588362" target="_blank" rel="noopener">HCA Magazine</a>.</p>
        <ol style="font-size:0.9rem;">
          <li>Which of your predictions and solutions matched what Uber did?</li>
          <li>Explain each change using delayering, span of control, chain of command, and centralization or decentralization.</li>
          <li>Uber merged its three Delivery Operations groups into global, regional and country-level teams. More or less centralized? Explain.</li>
          <li>Explain one new problem for the managers who remain.</li>
          <li>The articles mention losing a pipeline of future leaders. What should Uber do?</li>
          <li>Plan a 10-mark answer: discuss whether delayering was an appropriate response for Uber.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 5: Concept checks</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">1. A bakery has 12 staff who all report to the owner, who approves every purchase, price and schedule. Tall or flat? Centralized or decentralized?</div>
        <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Flat (two levels, span of 12). Centralized (the owner decides everything)</span></button>
      </div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">2. Name the structure: (a) one unit per store in five countries; (b) divisions for shoes, bags and watches; (c) marketing, finance, IT and operations departments.</div>
        <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">(a) by region (b) by product (c) by function</span></button>
      </div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">3. A company has a CEO and three regional heads. Sketch the top of the chart. Tall or flat? What else would you need to know?</div>
        <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">You can't tell from the top two levels: you'd need the number of levels below and the spans of control</span></button>
      </div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">4. Sales fall by 30% in a recession. Explain two ways the business might change its structure.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">5. A new payroll system automates most admin work. How might the structure change?</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 6: Structure and change</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">Zappos: an online retailer wants to respond faster to customers and encourage new ideas. What structure would you suggest, and why? Then read the Zappos case in your textbook (section 2.2.3). Why did Zappos decentralize, and to what extent was it appropriate?</div>
      </div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">Nova Snacks sells chips, noodles and juice in Vietnam and is about to expand into Thailand and Malaysia. Draw the top two levels of its chart (a) by product and (b) by region. Which suits the expansion better? Quick-draw it on <a href="https://unisam.nz/wb" target="_blank" rel="noopener">unisam.nz/wb</a>.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">For each change, pick a structure change and say why in one sentence. Options: delayer, decentralize, add regional units, add a project team, outsource, organize by product, remove or downsize a branch.</div>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Change</th><th>Structure change</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Recession: sales fall by 25%</td><td><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Delayer, or remove/downsize a branch, to cut costs</span></button></td></tr>
            <tr><td>An AI tool handles most customer queries</td><td><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Delayer or downsize customer service: fewer staff and supervisors needed</span></button></td></tr>
            <tr><td>The company opens in three new countries</td><td><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Add regional units so each market has local decision makers</span></button></td></tr>
            <tr><td>A rival launches new products every month</td><td><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Decentralize or add project teams to develop products faster</span></button></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <div class="callout remember">
          <div class="callout-title">Pending teacher review</div>
          <p>These suggested answers are pending teacher review. Other answers can be right if justified.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div><!-- /practice -->

